#!/bin/bash
# 702 (Sat 21 Nov 2026 15:53, Peter: "your rules are terrible ... review the data again and figure out the way to make money").
# Builder session 10. (1) Failed rules come off the paper book (his 19 Nov order: failed rules do not keep running).
# (2) The one thing every report today points to - being the one who OFFERS the price - built and tested properly on all days.
# Paper only. No orders. Nothing deleted.
B=/root/pe-builder
cat > $B/BRIEF_offer.md <<'BRIEF'
You are the Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 10. Started once. Do the job, leave ordinary background processes running, write one page, stop. Read first: /root/pe-queue/reports/builder_handover_7.md, losses_to_profit.md, exits_why.md, paper_breakdown.md and /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md (HARD RULES unchanged: paper only, never an order endpoint, nothing deleted, back up before editing). Reuse /root/pe-sports/grid/.
WHERE WE ARE, honestly: about 9,600 rules that TAKE a price have been tested; as a group they lose the cost of trading (about £2.30 per £100) on every half; the paper book is minus £10,068 in three days; starting from the winners produced 0 rules that hold. Three separate reports say the same thing: the steady winner is whoever OFFERS the price, and taking prices on racing before the off is already within 0.5 per £100 of level. Peter is rightly angry. Do not hand back another table of rules. Find out, properly, whether offering prices makes money for a small trader, and say yes or no with the number.
PART 1 - CLEAR THE PAPER BOOK (do this first, 20 minutes): every rule on paper that fails the honesty check (profit on both halves of ALL recorded days AND still in profit without its best day and its three biggest winners) comes off paper now: mark it 'failed' with the reason and the date in its own table and stop the paper trader placing for it. Its past paper trades stay in the record. Peter's standing preference: no lay at 5.0 or longer - those come off too. Say how many were on, how many stay, and name the ones that stay in plain sentences. From now the 'Trades' file shows only rules still on paper.
PART 2 - OFFERING PRICES, done as a real market maker would, not as 'a rule with an offer in it'. Simulate, on every recorded day (include 20 and 21 Nov straight from the live tables - do not wait for the cache), a quoter that:
 - works one market at a time, before the start only (no in-play: Betfair delays in-play bets and we do not model it);
 - rests a BACK order above the best lay-side price and a LAY order below the best back-side price so that it sits at or inside the gap, small size (£10, £25, £50 variants), on selections priced under 5.0 only for lays (Peter does not lay long shots; backs may be offered at any price);
 - is filled only as a real order would be: it joins the END of the queue at its price (queue = money shown at that price when it arrives) and fills only from money traded at that price after the queue ahead of it has been eaten, or immediately in full when the market trades THROUGH its price. Report results under both this queue model and the stricter through-only model; the truth is between them;
 - when one side fills, works the other side to close: moves its closing order with the market, and has a hard stop (out by taking the price when the position is down 1, 2 or 3 ticks - each a variant) and a hard time limit (flat by 60, 30 or 10 seconds before the off - each a variant; never carries a position into the race or match unless the variant says so, and show that variant separately as a BET);
 - never holds more than one open position per market and no more than £50 at risk in it;
 - pays 2% commission on each market's net winnings.
 Run it on: UK and Irish horse racing win markets in the last 30, 10, 5 and 2 minutes; greyhounds if recorded; football match odds, over/under and correct score in the last hour, split by how much money the market has; tennis match odds before the start; anything else recorded before the start. Split every result by: gap in ticks when the quote went in (1, 2, 3+), money traded in the market, price band, minutes to the start, whether the price was trending in the last 2 minutes.
 What matters and must be on the page: how often BOTH sides fill (a round trip) against one side only; what a round trip earns; what a one-sided fill costs when stopped; the net per £100 turned over and per market; orders a day; £ a day at £10, £25 and £50; both halves by date; the worst day; the result without the best day. The known danger is being filled only when the price is about to move against you: measure it directly (price 30 s and 2 min after each fill, against the fill).
 If any variant is in profit on both halves, under BOTH fill models, and still in profit without its best day: it goes onto paper as a live quoter in the paper trader (same fill models, forward only), and say exactly which markets, sizes, stop and time limit. If none: say so in the first line, give the nearest, and say what single thing stands between it and profit (the gap is too thin, the queue too long, adverse moves too big, commission).
WRITE /root/pe-queue/reports/offering_prices.md, plain English, one screen. Line 1: YES or NO - does offering prices make money here, and £ a day at £25 on the days we have. Line 2: where (sport, minutes before the start, kind of market). Line 3: what is now on paper. Then the tables. Also /root/pe-queue/reports/builder_handover_10.md under 20 lines (Part 1 counts first). Commit and push: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/offering_prices.md reports/builder_handover_10.md reports/trades_today.md reports/paper_forward.md 2>/dev/null; git commit -qm "builder session 10: paper book cleared; offering prices" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Push once after Part 1 and again at the end. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > $B/run_builder_offer.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/grid && timeout -k 60 21600 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_offer.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 600 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_offer.log 2>&1
echo "builder offer session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
SH
chmod +x $B/run_builder_offer.sh
if pgrep -f '[r]un_builder' >/dev/null; then echo "a builder session is still running - waiting"; exit 75; fi
nohup setsid $B/run_builder_offer.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & sleep 20; echo "offer session started: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_builder_offer.sh') at $(date -u +%H:%M) UTC"
